# syscall_x86_64_linux.cyr — x86_64 wrapper backfill for stdlib gaps
#
# Self-gated with `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` so this file ships in the bundled
# `dist/phylax.cyr` alongside the aarch64 peer; only the matching arch's block
# compiles in any given consumer build.
#
# ⚠ The guard is `CYRIUS_ARCH_X86`, the name the toolchain defines (as the
# stdlib's own peers use it). The 1.2.6 closeout emptied this file and, in the
# same edit, respelled the guard `CYRIUS_ARCH_X86_64` — which no cyrius from
# 6.5.35 to 6.7.9 defines. Inert only because the block was empty; anything
# added under that name would have been silently dropped on x86. Restored at
# 1.3.0.
#
# ⚠ CURRENTLY EMPTY BY DESIGN — do not delete the file.
#
# It used to define `sys_stat`, `sys_fstat` and `sys_rename`; the cyrius 6.5.x
# stdlib provides all three (removed at the 1.2.6 P(-1) closeout). As of 1.3.0
# the aarch64 peer is empty too — the stdlib's aarch64 `sys_rename` composes
# `renameat` exactly as phylax's copy did. Both peers stay in `[lib]` as the
# home for the next per-arch gap, which must be an arch-gated FUNCTION, never a
# `var SYS_* = <number>`: a global of that name beats the stdlib's per-target
# enum value program-wide, inside the stdlib's own wrappers too (see the note
# in src/types.cyr and tests/test_platform.tcyr).

#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86

#endif
